Synthesize the following 3 reviews into a meta-review:

- {"summary": "Q-RAG fine-tunes a retriever embedder with value-based RL for multi-step retrieval, using state/action embedders with (relative) RoPE and a PQN-style TD learner. It targets ultra-long contexts and reports SOTA on BabiLong and RULER, with competitive HotPotQA/MuSiQue results, while training on a single A100. An inner-product theory (App. A) supports the scoring class; early-stopping analysis is provided.", "significance_and_novelty": "Training the embedder (not the LLM) with value-based RL for multi-step retrieval is a useful efficiency-oriented twist, and the relative positional scheme for temporal reasoning is interesting. Novelty is moderate vis-\u00e0-vis RePlug/BeamRetriever/RL-agent lines.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 3.2: A clear, efficient value-based formulation with state/action embedders and dot-product Q enables scalable multi-step retrieval.", "Section 3.3: The relative position encoding tied to already-selected facts is a neat, well-motivated addition for temporal/narrative tasks.", "Section 4.3\u20134.4 and App. B: Early-stopping analysis and OOD MuSiQue results indicate some robustness beyond in-distribution tuning.", "Appendix D,G: Complexity analysis and practical training details (single A100) make the approach attractive for practitioners."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Section 3.2 mischaracterizes Appendix A as providing \u201cconvergence guarantees,\u201d but App. A contains approximation results for inner-product classes, not RL convergence, weakening the theoretical claim.", "Section 5 (Table 3) ablation values are missing (shown as \u201c. \u00b1 .\u201d), preventing verification of the benefit of Soft-Q, target networks, and RL vs SFT, which directly affects claimed contributions.", "Section 4.2\u20134.3: Central SOTA claims rely heavily on reported numbers from disparate setups; Table 1 is incomplete and unclear (missing columns/entries), impeding a fair, controlled comparison at ICLR standards.", "Clarity issues throughout (e.g., missing equations around soft Q definitions in \u00a73.2, broken tables/figures, numerous formatting/grammar issues) reduce confidence in correctness and reproducibility despite the code link."], "suggestions": ["Appendix D: Add a wall-clock throughput/memory comparison against BeamRetriever and ANN-backed retrieval with and without PQN to substantiate efficiency claims.", "Section 3.3: Provide an explicit ablation isolating absolute vs relative positional indexing to quantify temporal-reasoning gains.", "Section 4.4: Report results with a standard public answerer and decoding settings to ease comparison, and include sensitivity to retrieval budget on HotPotQA/MuSiQue in the main text.", "Appendix G: Release evaluation scripts that compute Fact EM/F1 exactly as defined, plus seeds and data splits, to align with open-review scrutiny."], "rating": 5, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 2, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes Q-RAG, a value-based RL method that fine-tunes a retriever\u2019s embedder for multi-step RAG. It builds on PQN with soft-Q and target networks and a factorized Q for scalable selection. Experiments on BabiLong, RULER (NIAH), HotPotQA, and MuSiQue show strong long-context generalization (up to 1M tokens) and competitive open-domain QA, including best OOD MuSiQue. Ablations support design choices.", "significance_and_novelty": "Applies on-policy value-based RL (PQN + soft-Q + targets) to train a retriever, addressing replay-buffer issues and scaling to long contexts, with empirical SOTA on long-context stress tests and strong OOD QA; novelty is incremental relative to prior RL-tuned retrievers.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 3.2 introduces a clear value-based RL formulation (PQN with soft value functions and target networks) and a factorized Q scoring, addressing replay-buffer inefficiency in large action spaces.", "Section 4.3/Table 1 shows near-perfect NIAH and generalization from training at 4K to evaluation at 1M tokens without accuracy loss.", "Section 4.4 and Appendix E report strong open-domain QA, matching Search-R1 and outperforming alternatives on MuSiQue OOD; Plan Q-RAG performs similarly.", "Section 5 ablations (with three seeds) indicate benefits of soft-Q and target networks and analyze sensitivity to \u03bb and \u03b1."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Section 4.4 claims Q-RAG \u201csurpasses all other baselines on HotPotQA,\u201d but Appendix E shows Beam Retriever slightly higher on HotPotQA (Ans F1 0.77 vs 0.76; Ans EM 0.61 vs 0.59; Fact metrics), making the claim overstated.", "Section 4.4 states baseline numbers were taken from prior papers, and G (Evaluation Details) uses QwQ-32B only for some methods; generator/config mismatches undermine apples-to-apples comparisons.", "G (Fact-level metrics) defines Fact EM as gt\u2286pred, which does not penalize extra irrelevant chunks and can overstate retrieval quality when predictions are long.", "Section 5 ablations are confined to BabiLong-QA3; there is no ablation on open-domain QA to show that soft-Q/targets consistently drive gains there."], "suggestions": ["Add qualitative analyses of retrieval trajectories and typical failure modes to illuminate what the value function learns (Section 3.2/Appendix C).", "Report sensitivity to the retrieval step limit T across datasets beyond BabiLong (G Evaluation Details).", "Summarize the main assumptions and theorem statements from Appendix A in the main text (Section 3.2) to improve accessibility.", "Release code and training scripts to facilitate reproducibility of the multi-seed ablations (Section 5/G)."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes Q-RAG, a multi-step retrieval method that trains the embedder with value-based TD reinforcement learning and a temporal chunk-position encoding, targeting long-context and multi-hop QA. It reports strong results on BabiLong QA3 and RULER, and competitive HotPotQA/MuSiQue performance, with ablations on Soft-Q/target networks and early stopping. Code and checkpoints are released; training fits on a single A100.", "significance_and_novelty": "Training the retriever via TD-RL with a temporal encoding is novel relative to SFT/agentic multi-step RAG and beam retrievers, and the claimed ultra-long-context generalization is potentially impactful if validated.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Sec. 4.2 reports strong, stable performance on BabiLong QA3 across increasing context length where competing long-context methods degrade.", "Sec. 4.4 and App. E show competitive fact/answer scores on HotPotQA and OOD MuSiQue, matching Search-R1 and approaching Beam Retriever without LLM fine-tuning.", "Sec. 5 ablations (Table 3, Fig. 3) indicate the necessity of Soft-Q, target networks, \u03bb-return and entropy regularization, supporting the methodological choices.", "Reproducibility statement and App. G provide code, checkpoints, hyperparameters, and consistent generation settings, suggesting the work is replicable on a single A100."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Abstract and Sec. 4.3 conflict on context length claims (\"up to 10M tokens\" vs experiments shown up to 1M), and no table/figure for 10M was available to verify the SOTA claim.", "Sec. 4.4 states baseline numbers were taken from papers and Sec. 4.1 notes resource limits, while App. G uses QwQ-32B for answers in our runs, creating potential evaluation non-comparability with reported baselines that may use different generators.", "Sec. 3.1 indicates all experiments rely on support-fact supervision rather than answer-level rewards, which limits applicability to settings lacking annotated supporting facts.", "Sec. 3.3 outlines a temporal positional encoding but omits a precise formulation and complexity analysis, and Appendix A introduces a Q-function approximation framework whose practical role in the implementation is unclear."], "suggestions": ["App. G: add sensitivity analyses to chunk size and sentence-boundary handling to document robustness beyond the chosen fixed segmentation.", "Sec. 3.3: include qualitative case studies visualizing temporal decisions over S_t to illustrate how temporal encoding guides retrieval.", "Sec. B: report the distribution of stopping times and threshold calibration curves across datasets to aid deployment tuning."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 2, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}
